\\nNo API key was available and no external calls were made, so I verified with a\\nharness that simulates prefix-cache semantics (cacheable chunks end at each\\n`cache_control` marker; the cached prefix includes the tools array; chunks\\nkeyed by exact content) and drives the real `reply()` loop through the note's\\nscenario — a message, a message that triggers `save_fact`, then a follow-up —\\nfor both the old single-block `build_system` and the new one. The harness\\nredirected `data/athletes.json` and `logs/usage.jsonl` to temp copies, so the\\nrepo's data and logs were not touched (confirmed afterward).\\n\\nResult (token counts approximate, `~len/4`):\\n\\n| call | BEFORE (one marker) | AFTER (two markers) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| msg1 round 1 | create 777 | create 781 |\\n| msg1 round 2 | read 777 | read 781 |\\n| msg2 round 1 (the `save_fact` call) | read 777 | read 781 |\\n| **msg2 round 2 (right after the write)** | **read 0, create 781** | **read 621, create 167** |\\n| msg3 follow-up | read 781 | read 788 |\\n\\nStatic instructions ≈ 621 tokens. After a memory write, the static block is\\nstill read from cache and only the ~167-token tail (context + tools) is\\nre-created — versus a full ~781-token re-cache before. Follow-up messages and\\ntool-loop rounds inside the TTL still read the whole prompt at cache price.\\nAlso `python3 -c \\\"import bot\\\"` passes without the anthropic SDK (the file's\\nfallback wrapper still works).\\n\\nTo confirm against the live API later: send two messages within 5 minutes\\n(second must show `cache_read_input_tokens` ≈ whole prompt), trigger a memory\\nwrite, then send a third — `cache_read` must still cover the static block and\\n`cache_creation` must be roughly tail-sized only.\\n\\n## Which parts of the colleague's note applied\\n\\nApplied:\\n\\n- **The core fix: two `cache_control: ephemeral` markers** — block 1 = static\\n  instructions, block 2 = volatile per-user tail (date, profile, zones, goal,\\n  facts, language). This is exactly what was implemented.\\n- **Byte-stability requirement for block 1** — audited `INSTRUCTIONS`: no\\n  interpolation, no counters; safe as the static segment. The note's warning\\n  about hunting interpolated values in the \\\"static\\\" block was checked.\\n- **Keep tools byte-stable across calls** — already satisfied by the existing\\n  keyword-filtered tool selection (core set always included, list constant\\n  within a message's tool loop), so it was left untouched.\\n- **Reject per-message personalization of cached content** (top-N fact\\n  selection) — never existed in this codebase and was not added; all facts are\\n  injected every message so the tail cache can hit.\\n- **Read `usage` from every response** — `log_usage` already records\\n  `cache_creation_input_tokens` / `cache_read_input_tokens`; used both the\\n  existing log and the harness output to verify.\\n\\nNot applied / not applicable:\\n\\n- **Tool filtering as a win** — the note describes adding keyword-based tool\\n  groups as its first win; this codebase already had it (NOTES.md dates it to\\n  March). Nothing to do.\\n- **The note's tools-above-system ordering nuance** — the concern that pulling\\n  in an extra tool group re-reads everything once per message is inherent to\\n  prefix caching and accepted; the default core tool set covers most messages,\\n  and it is not a regression from this change.\\n- **Reordering the tail \\\"rarest-changing first\\\"** — the note itself calls this\\n  readability-only (\\\"after the marker it's one hash anyway\\\"), so the existing\\n  `athlete_context` order was kept to minimize churn.\\n- **The 4-marker allowance** — we use 2 of 4, as the note recommends; no\\n  third marker was needed since the tools array is already byte-stable per\\n  message.\\n\"}"}}]}
